 "paragraphs": [
  {
   "type": "prose",
   "segments": [
    {
     "id": "p001.s01",
     "source": "There was once a gentleman that lived in a very grand house, and he married a young lady that had been delicately brought up."
    },
    {
     "id": "p001.s02",
     "source": "In her husband’s house she found everything that was fine—fine tables and chairs, fine looking-glasses, and fine curtains; but then her husband expected her to be able to spin twelve hanks o’ thread every day, besides attending to her house; and, to tell the even-down truth, the lady could not spin a bit."
    },
    {
     "id": "p001.s03",
     "source": "This made her husband glunchy with her, and, before a month had passed, she found hersel’ very unhappy."
    }
   ],
   "id": "p001"
  },
  {
   "type": "prose",
   "segments": [
    {
     "id": "p002.s01",
     "source": "One day the husband gaed away upon a journey, after telling her that he expected her, before his return, to have not only learned to spin, but to have spun a hundred hanks o’ thread."
    },
    {
     "id": "p002.s02",
     "source": "Quite downcast, she took a walk along the hillside, till she cam’ to a big flat stane, and there she sat down and grat."
    },
    {
     "id": "p002.s03",
     "source": "By and by she heard a strain o’ fine sma’ music, coming as it were frae aneath the stane, and, on turning it up, she saw a cave below, where there were sitting six wee ladies in green gowns, ilk ane o’ them spinning on a little wheel, and singing,"
    }
   ],
   "id": "p002"
  },
  {
